<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lucretia Mott</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1793">1793</a>–<a href="/years/1880">1880</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucretia">Lucretia</a> Mott was an American Quaker, abolitionist, women's rights activist and social reformer. Excluded with other women from the World Anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> Convention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1840, she formed the idea of reforming the position of women in society, and in 1848 Jane Hunt invited her to the meeting that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-falls-convention">Seneca Falls Convention</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-sentiments">Declaration of Sentiments</a> was written. A Quaker preacher in her early adulthood, she advocated the vote for black people, both male and female, and her home with her husband James Mott was a stop on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad</a>. She helped found the Female Medical College of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> and Swarthmore College.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lucretia-mott.jpg" alt="Lucretia Mott" width="330" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frederick Gutekunst / Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucretia_Mott,_signed_photo,_by_F._Gutekunst.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Anti-Slavery Convention in London in 1840 refused to seat the American women delegates, among them the Quaker minister <b>Lucretia Mott</b>, she and the young Elizabeth Cady Stanton, who was in London on her honeymoon, resolved, by Stanton’s later account, to hold a convention on the rights of women.</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In the United States the early campaigners <b>Lucretia Mott</b>, Elizabeth Cady Stanton and Susan B. Anthony, all opponents of slavery first, drew on the Quaker theology of spiritual equality, which holds men and women equal under God, and on earlier writers such as Judith Sargent Murray, John Neal,…</blockquote><a href="/ideology/feminism">Feminism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Elizabeth Cady Stanton and <b>Lucretia Mott</b> opened the first women's rights convention in the United States.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-19">19 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantucket">Nantucket</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.28,-70.09&amp;year=1849" aria-label="Nantucket on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2838 692 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lucretia Mott"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3288 962h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3288 962h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.28,-70.09&amp;year=1849">What was here in 1849? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
